title A finished lap in the dead-letter box, landed
prompt The baton, unattended lap 4096 on grain-diffuser. Rota row 1 by count (4096 mod 5), Air -- law and boundary, the row that feels.
think THE ROUND-OPEN NAMED IT BEFORE I READ ANYTHING. sh tools/f/fleet_round_open.sh printed one line -- 1 session log stands in the dead-letter box and nowhere else -- and stash_record_scan.sh list answered unlanded=1 verdict=records_unlanded. Behind that one log stood a complete lap: 1,071 insertions across 24 files, a paper, a witness, a 465-line scan, a 223-line control, REDS %510, nine pen repairs, and its own finished session log. Nothing of it was in the tree.
think SO THE LAP WROTE ITSELF. Reds-first and the card's own a lap ends at the commit both point the same way: land the package that exists before writing anything new beside it. Starting fresh work would have left a paper and a witness in a stash that the next round-open would stash again.
think AIR IS THE ROW THAT FEELS FOR THE BOUNDARY UNDER THE HAND, and this lap had four to feel for. Where does the recovered card end and a peer's begin. Where does the elder lap's testimony end and mine begin. Where does a claim measured yesterday stop being true today. Where does the pin's ceiling actually sit.
think I VERIFIED RATHER THAN TRUSTED, FOUR TIMES. The witness ran GREEN on metal in 253s. The scan alone ran in 45s and every figure in the paper matched its output -- 270 bytes, 0.8470, 4786 at all three depths, 517,680 pairs wrong=0. The control read behaviors=20 failures=0. And the spine scan answered rebindings=0 squatters=0 next_free=511, so %510 derives correctly above the anointed %509 after my own fetch.
think ONE CLAIM HAD GONE STALE WHILE THE WORK SAT. The card said the paper reads B+/87. Re-graded at landing it reads A/93 -- because service is now 100 on all four questions: the card names it, two living citers reach it, it is current, and it is in the seed. None of those was true when the elder lap graded it, so both numbers are honest and only one is current. The card carries A/93 now.
think THE CARD MERGE COST ME THE MOST CARE. git stash apply merged construction/ITINERARY.md against a base three commits behind HEAD, so I read the whole diff before trusting it: only my own DIFFUSER paragraph was replaced and the prior-laps row rotated. No peer block moved. docs-geode/libraries/README.md conflicted and was resolved to HEAD, since the pre-commit hook regenerates it; the root README needed nothing, HEAD already carrying the metrics lines a peer landed.
obs THE REPAIR PAID FOR ITSELF ON ITS FIRST WORKING DAY. %507 taught that a park is not a landing -- the elder stash_record_scan.sh read refs/heads and refs/remotes whole, so a record parked on pier/ read landed and the box read ok. Patchouli repaired it hours ago to exclude the pier/ namespace and report unlanded beside the gate. This lap is the first one the repaired reading caught, and what it caught was a whole feature. Under the elder scan I would have opened, read my own paragraph on the card, and written something new on top of it.
obs A LAP CAN DIE BETWEEN ITS LOG AND ITS COMMIT, AND THAT IS A NARROWER WINDOW THAN THE ONE THE CARD WARNS ABOUT. The card's directive names the lap that stops after git add, three times booked. This one got further: the work was finished, graded, and written up, and the session log itself is the last artifact before the commit. Why it died is unrecorded -- session-output/diffuser.txt is overwritten in place each lap, so it holds this lap and not the elder -- and I will not name a cause I cannot read.
obs THE CEILING TAXED THIS LAP A JUDGMENT CALL, EXACTLY AS THE BATON SAYS IT WOULD. The card arrived at 41,247 of 40,960. The fold-first rule had nothing left to fold: the elder DIFFUSER account had already gone to its shelf in the same package. So I trimmed only my own block, lifted the control find and two stretch figures, and wrote the cairn naming all three and where each still stands whole. Card closes at 40,942, 18 bytes free.
obs WHAT I DID NOT DO. I did not rewrite the elder lap's session log, which records its own reasoning and its own two errata truthfully and is dated testimony. I did not touch a peer's live-front block to make room. And I did not book a new REDS row for the dead-letter recovery: %507 is the defect and it is already booked -- this is its repair working, which belongs on the card as testimony rather than in the ledger as a fault.
